  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nDry conditions will prevail for the remainder of the day as a weak \nridge builds over the region. Tonight areas of patchy fog will again \nbe possible with light winds and a moist boundary layer. A round of \nshowers/thunderstorms is expected to develop overnight/early \nThursday morning across IA and southwest WI along the noise of a \nstrengthening LLJ. There is still a lot of spread amounts the latest \nCAMs as to how morning convection will play out exactly, but a \nparameter space of ~1500 J/kg and 40-45kt 0-6km shear along and just \nnorth of I-90 does point to at least a low (10-20% chance) for \nstrong to severe storms with up to 1 inch hail and 40-60 mph wind \ngusts as these storms move northeastward through central and east-\ncentral WI. A second round of thunderstorms is possible Thursday \nafternoon/evening as a compact mid-level shortwave moves across the \nregion. CAMs suggest initially discrete cells develop over ND/MN with \nthe potential to grow upscale into an eastward propagating MCS \nduring the afternoon. If an organized MCS is able to develop would \nexpect it to track along the instability gradient from east central \nWI toward northern IL. This would put parts of the forecast area, \nsouth of a Merrill to Green Bay line in play, however, there is still \na lot of uncertainty with how convection will evolve through the \nafternoon and evening.\n\nThe warm and humid airmass will continue to linger over the area \nFriday through this weekend keeping on and off chances for showers \nand storms each day. However, confidence in the timing of any \nprecipitation is low as forcing remains rather nebulous due to the \nlack of stronger synoptic features.",
